Cannibal-Fretwork

Ingredients: DANCE-PUNK & NU RAVE + TROPICALIA + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + Viola
Creative Catalyst: Its first record was cut aboard a generation ship halfway to Proxima Centauri.

Cannibal-Fretwork sounds like an industrial sabotage ritual held inside a hydro-organic greenhouse spinning out of orbit. Screaming electro-acoustic violas are played with serrated fiberglass bows, tearing through hyper-syncopated cowbell-heavy dance-punk disco grooves and glitched Brazilian psychedelic sambas. The vocals are feral, distorted multi-lingual chants that alternate between rabid multi-vocal snarls and warped, pitch-shifted tropical harmonies, drenched in metallic laser-strobe delays and recycled oxygen hiss.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 128–142 BPM. Unforgiving, four-on-the-floor kick drums collided with frantic 16th-note agogô bell polyrhythms and violent, off-beat hi-hat stabs.
- Harmonic Palette: Tropicalist Lydian and Mixolydian melodies dragged aggressively into minor-second microtonal friction, leaning heavily on screeching pitch-shifted viola dissonances and distorted acid basslines.
- Production Signatures: "Vacuum-Crush" dynamic ducking (simulating bulk-head airlock depressurization), microphonic pickup overload on wooden violas, and zero-gravity re-amping through metal hull corridors.
- Dynamics: Abrupt, violent drops from blistering multi-instrumental rave chaos into bone-dry, claustrophobic acoustic viola scraping, followed by sudden explosion-level beat re-engagements.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the claustrophobic agro-bays of the generation vessel SS Osvaldo de Andrade during the mid-flight "Decade of Rot," Cannibal-Fretwork emerged when third-generation hydroponic laborers rebelled against artificial rationing. Deprived of standard electric guitars, the rioters retrofitted heirloom acoustic violas with piezoelectric engine-monitors, blasting their frantic noise through the ship's emergency PA system. Dressed in reflective biohazard suits slashed open to reveal neon-dyed Brazilian lace and body paint made from crushing bioluminescent algae, the subculture—known as As Traças (The Moths)—pioneered zero-G mosh pits. They perform standing under hyper-strobe UV lamps, swigging fermented algae-wash, and violently cutting their clothes with shears mid-dance to represent shedding the physical hull that imprisons them.

Instruments
- Amplified Serrated-Bow Viola: An acoustic viola equipped with contact mics, played using bowed fiberglass coated in industrial grit to produce high-register, razor-sharp textures.
- Vacuum-Tube Acid Synth: Analog bass synthesizer routing squelching squiggles through high-gain distortion pedals originally used for signal telemetry.
- Agogô-Bateria Synth Rig: Electronic pads loaded with samples of metallic hull-clangs, tropical percussion, and overdriven disco cowbells.
- Laser-Gated Vocal Modulator: A hardware vocal processor that chops human screams into rhythmic 16th-note pitch-warped stabs.

